he commit lands and that upload is ended together with the part it has just committed. Closing that needs a change to the upload protocol (proposal 0016) that this release does not make; it is a known gap, and the follow-up that closes it must land before uploads are created in production. Each commit is measured against the batch budgets before it is sent and never split. An operator can run exactly one such pass and exit with `wyrd custodian --reap-abandoned-uploads`: it needs no `--endpoints`, reads and writes metadata only, and drains what it ended record by record, holding for a whole-fleet pass anything that could only be retired by listing the fleet; it exits non-zero when anything was held, a commit's outcome is unknown or the profile check refused, so an open upload under another clock keeps it failing until an operator ends that upload. It is refused before it opens the store when given a flag that selects work it does not run — `--endpoints`, `--ids`, `--failure-domains` or `--gc-expired-pending` — rather than ignoring the flag, and when its `--data-dir` holds no local metadata store — no file there, or an empty one — which it never creates: a store made on the spot would be reported as holding nothing to reap. Its last line names the store it ran on: the local file, or the TiKV PD endpoints or the FoundationDB cluster file it connected with.
+
+   An upload the reaper will not judge — one stamped under a clock it does not read — is ended by an **operator abort**: `wyrd mpu abort --upload-id ID [--data-dir DIR] [--metadata-backend B]` (proposal 0016's operator exit, FU-6). It names exactly one upload, by its id — a second `--upload-id`, a pattern or a wildcard is refused, and so is any flag it does not take, a clock source among them, since it judges no clock — and it acts on the store the cluster wrote, never creating one. Its command line is read strictly, before the store is opened: each flag at most once and with a value, a value that is empty or spelled as a flag (`--data-dir --bogus`) refused rather than taken, and `--data-dir` refused on a networked backend, which takes its address from the environment and would ignore it. An **open** upload is moved to aborting with the obligation owing everything it staged, the same commit a client abort makes. An upload **completing** — a publication fenced and stopped — is moved to aborting with that obligation and a second one owing the segment records its attempt wrote, the range of that attempt alone and never the upload's later or earlier attempts, the same commit the post-restore pass makes for such an upload. Each is one commit, conditioned on the upload's exact record and on the obligations being absent, so no state has the upload aborting with something it holds that nothing will reclaim. The abort deletes nothing, writes no mark and leaves the admission count alone: the teardown is the drain's, which reads no clock, so the operator runs `wyrd custodian --reap-abandoned-uploads` after it, or leaves it to the deployed custodian's next pass. The abort prints that one-shot for the store it acted on — the backend by name and, for redb, the directory, quoted for a shell — so the line can be run as printed. An upload already aborting is left as it is and the abort exits 0; a **completed** upload is refused, since abort does not apply to a terminal session, and so is an upload with no record, each with exit 1 and a message naming what it found. A commit that loses to a change of the record is answered from what the record now holds, and a commit whose outcome the store could not report is settled by reading the record again, claiming no more than that read shows: found moved to aborting as that commit moves it, the abort reports the upload aborting, by its commit or by a rival one that writes the same record, exit 0; found gone, it reports the upload possibly fenced and already torn down, never that nothing was written, exit 1; found unchanged, it tries again. It makes a bounded number of commits, and if it stops with one of them possibly still landing it exits 1 saying so, so a re-run reads which way it went. This verb is an interim surface on the development CLI, granted until the management plane of proposal 0008 exists: its authority is host access to the metadata store, with no listener and no authentication path of its own, and it moves onto the management API when that lands, with the same semantics and the same records.
 3. **Propagate across zones (L3).** If the object had replicas, the deletion propagates through the replica catalog: each holding zone drops its catalog record and its GC reclaims the local fragments. Until propagation completes, a *stale-tolerant* read in a lagging zone may still see the old object, bounded by replication lag (section 8.1); consistency-sensitive reads route to the home zone and never do.
 4. **Prove it (audit + crypto-erase).** The deletion is recorded in the append-only audit/event log — the operator's compliance and GDPR-deletion story (section 8.3). Where per-tenant envelope encryption is enabled, dropping the object/tenant key crypto-shreds the data immediately, making it unrecoverable independent of when GC runs (section 8.5).
